[{:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the api writes an agent's [agent :in] with Flow's blocking send (64 slots plus a 1 MB socket, `seon.agent-proc`). Reproduced: one busy agent plus one chatty agent stalled root's whole api about 25 s while health said :running. Correct: the api never blocks on one agent (the agent proc holds overflow in its state and admits what fits); health reports progress (the oldest unanswered request), not liveness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "start-takes-a-config-layer"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.root/start!` takes `:max-running`, `:provider` and `:stall-ms` as options beside the config, and `seon.config/with-provider` folds a provider into the config written on main's root entity; bin/check (`:max-running 2 :stall-ms 1000`) and seon.e2e pass them. The written config is what the reconciler reads (main's root fact), so nothing bypasses main; outside the one resolver are the process's own reads: `seon.config/value` reads the process-wide `seon.config/config` (loaded once with its --set layers), so two roots in one process (the checks') cannot differ through it. Correct: a root's config in force is a layer the resolver reads (start! takes `{:set {k v}}`, resolved like a --set but per root; every reader in root's procs resolves through the root's config, not the process's); the options and with-provider go. A design change: before building, root's gate (AGENTS §3) asks which existing mechanism carries a per-root layer and what v1 did (a config per system)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-log-is-bounded"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/root start` sends a detached root's output to runs/root.log (bin/root), appended across every start for the checkout's life; nothing bounds or rotates it. Correct: when bin/root starts a root and the log is past a config key's bound (bytes, its unit and provenance in resources/seon/config/default.edn), it moves the log to root.log.1, replacing the one before, so a checkout keeps the current log and one previous. Rotation happens at start, an event, never on a timer. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-start-past-the-bound-keeps-one-previous-log}}
 {:seon.task/name "operator-commands"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/root` has status, start, stop and next; one root per checkout on its own ports (`bin/ports`); its output goes to runs/root.log. v1's bin/seon also had open (the dashboard in the browser), logs (the root's log) and reset --force (the running root unlinks its main and forks a fresh one, keeping every cache; ~/src/seon/script/seon/operator.clj). Correct: `bin/root open` opens the checkout's dashboard URL; `bin/root logs` follows runs/root.log; `bin/root reset` restarts the root on a new main of the same store (`:seon.config.root/main`), the old main kept as history and nothing deleted (deleting a record is the owner's act). Each a line in bin/root's usage and docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/reset-starts-a-new-main-and-keeps-the-old}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-prunes-old-generations-and-check-logs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: nothing prunes `target/check/` (each check run's logs) or a root's old generations (under `:seon.config.source/generations-dir`, each a worktree and a binary of about 200 MB, made by `seon.fx/build` with bin/worktree --at); deleting them is manual, keeping the running generation and the one before (docs/machine.md#disk-stays-bounded). Correct: root's merge, after the :build effect answers, removes every generation but the running one and the one before (bin/worktree --remove-at), and removes check logs past a config key's count of runs; a store holding a kept run is never removed (runs are evidence; reclaiming them is the owner's act). Deletes the manual step from docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-build-leaves-the-running-generation-and-the-one-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Jolt's Flow runs :compute steps on `clojure.core.async.impl.dispatch`'s compute-executor, an unbounded newCachedThreadPool of unnamed daemon threads with a 60 s keep-alive, and bounds each step with .get compute-timeout-ms 5000: a step past 5 s throws into root's errors while its worker keeps running, and the next step takes another worker. No proc in src declares :workload :compute today (`seon.render.proc/render-proc` is :mixed), so the hypothesis that it explains root's unattributed threads (lifecycle 25 -> 29 over cycles, scale 100 root threads 38 -> 55) is unproven. Correct: first prove or refute it (name the threads, or count compute workers before and after each cycle). If it holds, root passes create-flow a declared :compute-exec (a fixed pool of named threads, its size a seon.config key) and a step's bound becomes an observed overrun (its duration recorded), not an abandoned future. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle equals the declared sum, exactly."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares
                      seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-is-answered-and-failover-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the fork server's :standby-failed event (`seon.fork-server`, forwarded to the api) reaches `seon.root/back-op`, whose :ev case has no branch for it (read 2026-11-06: only seon.fork-server names :standby-failed) and falls through to the unchanged state: dropped. The server then has no standby, and its next death is :fork-server-lost with no recorded cause. Correct: a ping's answer passes through as itself (an error map stays an error map); :unknown only for a proc observed dead; a failed standby is a recorded platform problem (:seon.fork-server/standby-failed with :active-threads), and the view shows the server has no standby."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-ping-of-a-gone-agent-answers-its-error
                      seon.procs-test/a-failed-standby-is-a-recorded-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.agent/stall-ms` (30000, a guessed tolerance, now a config key; it was a var) is the silence after which a busy agent is observed stalled once; `seon.root/start!` takes :stall-ms, and the api's step schedules its stall check with a go block on `(a/timeout (quot stall-ms 2))` in seon.root. A 25 s compute shows nothing while a legitimate long proof run reads as stalled. Correct: a request carries its declared bound (the form's, :seon.config.agent/form-ticks; a test run's is its tick budget, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks`) and the agent proc observes a stall when that bound passes with no frame: one recorded :seon.agent/stalled naming the bound, no second number; the ticks come from the one :clock proc. Deletes stall-ms, the :stall-ms option and the go-block timer. The flusher's sleep this task also named is gone (durability is continuous group commit, AGENTS.md section 5)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-stall-is-observed-past-the-requests-own-bound
                      seon.procs-test/the-flusher-waits-on-the-clock}}
 {:seon.task/name "event-loss-is-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's buffers are literals: `seon.root/start!`'s events-in is `(a/sliding-buffer 8192)`, `seon.root/events` taps a sliding 1024 that is never untapped, and the render proc's tap is a sliding 4096 (seon.render.proc). A sliding buffer drops the oldest with no count, so the dashboard can miss events and nothing says so. Correct (the step-fn law: buffers bounded by their loss semantics): every buffer is declared in resources/seon/system.edn :chan-opts with its loss; a sliding buffer counts its drops into the api's view (:events-dropped by tap), shown on the dashboard; c/events taps are released with their reader. Estimate: one counter per drop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted
                      seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration}}
 {:seon.task/name "warm-exercises-the-render-and-request-path"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.root/warm!` (private; run once at boot before the fork server forks) runs d/q, d/pull, d/db-with and d/with on a value of its own, `seon.sock/frame`, `seon.print/print-value`, and a namespace's defn and deftest through clojure.test, but no render pass (`seon.render/walker`; the render proc's comp-entry and its component cache were deleted in 7e999e41bf) and not the conversation's request path (`seon.turn/context-block`), so a forked process's first render and first turn are cold. Correct: warm! runs each once on a seed value before the fork server forks. Proof: a forked agent's first turn costs what its second does (ticks), measured."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). seon.root/await-db parks on a promise that only a later commit satisfying pred delivers; its callers add a defect after t as a second end (seon_done, src/seon/server.clj), and test/seon/await.clj's await-quiet rides on it. When root is quiet (no message in flight, every proc parked: the reconciler's :seon.root/quiescent report) and pred does not hold, nothing inside root will ever make it hold, and the wait hangs its caller, the caller's socket and, in a test, the shard (instability 2: 1h45m at 0% CPU). Root stopping does not end it either. Correct, at the producer: root keeps its open waits (await-db's watch keys) and answers each, at the quiescence report that finds pred false, with a typed error {:seon.error/type :seon.root/never-true :t t :quiet-t q} (a fact, never a clock); and at stop! with :seon.root/stopped. A caller that waits on the outside world (a file change, a peer's commit) says so and is not answered at quiet (an explicit :outside? flag, the one exception). seon_done then answers a judge that never finished as that error. judge! puts with >!! on a 64-slot channel and parks forever on a dead, full consumer: the same rule, a put to a proc that died answers its error (see seon.root/a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). Proofs: an await-db whose pred cannot hold on a quiet root answers :seon.root/never-true; one waiting at stop! answers :seon.root/stopped; seon_done on a run whose judge's agent died answers the error, never parks. Checked 2026-11-06 at a root on this tree. Built for a named wait (seon.root/await-db with a description; proved by seon.procs-test/a-wait-on-main-is-answered-at-the-reconcilers-quiet, green): (seon.root/await-db sys \"never\" (fn [db] false)) answered {:seon.error/type :seon.root/quiet-before-true :t .. :waits-on \"never\"} with no commit, not :seon.root/never-true as written above, and a stopped root answers :seon.root/no-agent, not :seon.root/stopped. Not built: the unnamed arity is ended only by a commit; there is no :outside? flag; seon.root/judge! still puts with a/>!! and returns nil (task a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). No proof is named on this task. seon_done on a run whose judge agent died was not run."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). >!! onto a bounded channel whose consumer died parks the putter forever once the buffer is full: seon.root/judge! (src/seon/root.clj, 64 slots), src/seon/web.clj toward the web proc, src/seon/fork_server.clj's event reader into ev-ch. A Flow proc that dies leaves its in-channels open. Correct: a proc's death closes its in-channels (Flow's own stop closes them; a step that threw is the defect path), so a put returns false and the putter records the dead proc as its answer (AGENTS section 4: a dependency is a channel a proc parks on; its death is a fact). Proof: a proc stopped with a full in-channel; a >!! to it returns false and its caller answers an error naming the proc. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.root/judge! (src/seon/root.clj), the web proc (src/seon/web.clj, five a/>!! calls) and the fork server (src/seon/fork_server.clj, two, into ev-ch) still call a/>!! and drop its result; judge! returns nil. No test stops a proc with a full in-channel. Not run against a dead proc."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-named-wait-ends-when-a-runs-work-throws"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the agent loop's worktree session: with seon.turn/begin! writing a session's run on its branch without its task (fixed in jolt/agent-loop 89dca5cf3), a session's second run threw in seon.turn/work! (seon.ctx.status/task-data's contract, its task nil), and both stage tests' named waits (seon.root/await-db sys \"the implement parked\" ...; agent_test a-requests-implement-continues-its-designs-request and a-waiting-agent-parks-and-a-message-wakes-the-same-process) waited six hours: root's problems held the eval error, yet the named wait, which must end at the reconciler's quiet with :seon.root/quiet-before-true, never ended. Reproduction: revert 89dca5cf3's change to begin! and run bin/test seon.agent-test. Cause not yet found: a work! answered with :ex goes on to :judge and :branch-diff (seon.reconcile :worked), so either one of those requests is never answered on a run whose work! threw, or the run's end never reaches quiet. Correct: the run ends (its :worked-error as its reason) and the wait answers quiet-before-true; a test that waits on a fact the run cannot make goes red, never hangs. Checked 2026-11-06: seon.reconcile now ends a flight whose seon.turn/work! threw, with the reason \"seon.turn/work! threw: <message>\" and the error on the task (src/seon/reconcile.clj, the :worked-error flight key); the unit proof is the last testing block of seon.reconcile-test/a-death-names-its-cause. The proof named below is not written: no deftest of that name is in test/. The hang itself (revert 89dca5cf3, run bin/test seon.agent-test) was not re-run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-whose-work-throws-ends-and-the-wait-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner: we may have merge conflicts or agents failing to merge, so make sure the tools to fix them by hand on root's side work as expected. On a worktree root of one's own (never runs/store), stage each failure and repair it from root's REPL (seon_eval root, or the server's :eval and :attach ops) with only the tools that exist, as root would at 3 a.m.: (1) two branches change one var differently and the merge refuses (:seon.merge/unmerged) or its merge task cannot resolve it: list the conflicts (:seon.task/conflicts, my.root/problems, root's page), resolve (attach to the held merge task, then my.branch/unmerged and my.branch/resolve! :theirs or a hand redefinition), get it merged; (2) a run green on its branch whose merged program is red: find it from root, read why, send it back or fix it; (3) a run stuck :behind main, or a held task: see it on root's page, release or rerun it (seon.root/release!, my.root/run!, my.root/cancel!); (4) a write-back to git that fails (:seon.task/commit-error): see it, fix the cause, retry. Staging: the scripted scenarios in seon.fixtures/scenarios (:hairy and :conflict for 1, :semantic for 2, :hairy's held merges for 3), booted as test/seon/e2e.clj's scenario mode boots a scripted root; 4 by moving or locking the merges branch. Known before staging: root has no conflict-resolving function of its own; my.branch/unmerged, resolve! and merge-main! run in an agent's process, so root's path is to attach to the held merge task (whether that works end to end is finding 1's question). State: staged and repaired once on a worktree root (2026-10-27), every form and reply in docs/merging.md: conflicts resolve by attaching to the held merge task and resolve! :theirs; a held task releases with my.root/run!; red-after-merge is caught (the writer refuses a branch behind main). Gaps found, most severe first: (1) every worktree root writes back to the shared jolt/seon-merges ref (seon.fx/git-commit; one branch name per checkout, or refuse a ref another worktree has checked out); (2) a failed write-back is lost and never retried, the run reads :merged and no root cause names it (a my.root/write-back!, and a merged task with a :seon.task/commit-error as a root cause); (3) a merge stays :behind forever when main gained a namespace (the branch carries the form with :ns nil; seon.merge/merge-tx, program-of), and (4) nothing bounds :behind reruns (they count as green, so max-merge-turns never sees them); (5) attach leaves the task's model \"outside\" for good, so its next run idles; (6) no tool takes a running run back (run! refuses, release! does not check, cancel! is final; a my.root/hold!); (7) a behind merge branch is judged before merging main (\"proof-unknown\" instead of \"merge main first\"); (8) cancel leaves the task's problem entries open, and the page counts closed entries; (9) the page shows root causes only when quiet, my.root/problems allocates 0.1-1.7 GB a call on ~25 tasks, a request with raw newlines answers one contract error per line, a retried write-back keeps its old error. Next step: report these to root (done), then fix each at its cause on small branches (1 and 2 first), file the big ones, and write the working forms into docs/merging.md as root's playbook, one section per failure, each form runnable so the docs test can check it. Also staged: record per failure the forms root typed, what came back and whether root's page showed it; report the findings before fixing; fix each wrong or missing tool at its cause on a small branch (or file it if big); write the working forms into docs/merging.md as root's playbook, one section per failure, each form runnable so the docs test can check it. Checked 2026-11-06: of the gaps above, 1 is built (seon.config reads #seon/own-branch, so a worktree root commits on jolt/seon-merges-<worktree>), 2 is built (my.root/write-back!, docs/merging.md), 6 is built (my.root/hold!, proved by seon.reconcile-test/root-takes-a-running-task-back-and-releases-it-again, green). 3, 4 and 7 are the tasks a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact, 5 an-attached-run-is-taken-back-by-root, 9 root-repair-reads-are-cheap; 8 was not checked."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "await-db-has-one-arity"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S8; still true: seon.root/await-db has an unnamed arity ended only by a commit and a named one that also ends on the reconciler's quiet, src/seon/root.clj). A caller chooses whether the fact it waits on is the reconciler's work, and a named wait can answer :seon.root/quiet-before-true while another proc (the vector proc embedding) can still commit. Correct: one wait that ends when nothing in the system can still commit, from the quiet facts every proc reports (the reconciler's, the vector proc's, the problems report's :quiescent), no flag and no second arity. Replaces the two arities and the reconciler's held-waits bookkeeping beside root's quiescence report (seon.reconcile/quiet). Do with seon.root/a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true. Proof to write: a named wait during an embedding does not answer quiet-before-true."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-attached-run-is-taken-back-by-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 5 and 6 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27; 6 is built, below). (5) `seon.server/attach!` writes the task's own model as outside (task/env-tx id {:model outside}, server.clj) and nothing restores it: after seon_done the task still reads :seon.task/model outside, so a later run of it (a :behind respawn) idles with nothing driving it and quiet never comes. Correct: outside is the run's env only, or :done restores the task's model. (6) Built: `my.root/hold!` (src/my/root.clj; `my.root/take-back-tx` writes the compare-and-swap from :running to :held and the reconciler kills the live run) is proved by `seon.reconcile-test/root-takes-a-running-task-back-and-releases-it-again`, which exists. Gap 5 is the part left: docs/merging.md (the line on an attach) still says the task's model stays \"outside\" after an attach and to retract `:seon.task/model` before releasing it; the code comment on `seon.server/attach!` says the env's model is \"outside\" and the task keeps its own; which holds was not run. Proof to write: after done on an attached run the task reads its own model. Ran 2026-11-06 at a root on this tree: attach on seon.render/spend-has-a-render, then seon_done (the task closed). The task has no :seon.task/model before the attach, while attached (outcome :running) or after done; seon.server/attach! sets \"outside\" on the run env only (src/seon/server.clj, (assoc (r/desired-env t) :model \"outside\")). So gap 5 does not occur on that input; the docs/merging.md line that says the model stays \"outside\" and to retract :seon.task/model is not what the code does. A task that carries its own :seon.task/model was not tried. No proof is written."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 3, 4 and 7 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27, to reproduce first: stage fixtures :conflict, attach the merge task, merge main and resolve, then give main a new namespace with seon.source/text-tx). (a) A merge stays :behind forever after main gains a namespace: the form of a namespace new on main reaches the branch with :ns nil, so main differs at that key on every merge (`seon.merge/program-of` :ns read, merge.clj, or the carry in `seon.merge/merge-tx`, merge.clj); control: a change to an existing namespace merges on the second run. Fix at the carry; check a namespace made from an agent's REPL (the :newns scenario) the same way. (b) Nothing bounds :behind reruns: a :behind run ends green so max-merge-turns never counts it (reconcile.clj ends the run :behind and the task stays ready); under a model that loops forever and root causes (quiescence only) never names it. A :behind of a branch that already merged main at the head it was refused at is a defect fact, and the task is held with that evidence. (c) A behind merge branch is judged before main is merged: an attached agent's REPL runs main's program while its branch db holds the old base, so the proofs pass on the branch and the held reason reads proof-unknown. seon_done (or the judge) refuses a branch whose base is behind with the writer's :seon.merge/behind message and its fix before judging proofs, or attach to a merge task runs my.branch/merge-main! as the turn loop does. Proof to write: merge-main! followed by the octopus merge is not :behind when main gained a namespace; a :behind rerun at an unchanged head holds the task. Checked 2026-11-06: (a) is built and proved, in the direction a branch makes a namespace and main takes it, by seon.merge-test/a-merge-carries-the-namespace-of-a-form-it-carries (green); main making the namespace and the branch merging main (the scenario above) has no test. (b) is built as a count, not as unchanged-head: :seon.config.merge/max-behind (3) judgings of one merge task that end :behind hold it for root with the writer evidence (seon.reconcile-test/a-merge-behind-again-and-again-is-held-for-root, green); a rerun at an unchanged head is not held earlier. (c) is built for the explanation only: a behind branch is told \"behind N\" before proof-unknown (seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-behind-main-is-told-so-first, green); whether attach runs merge-main! was not checked. Tests and default.edn cite these as seon.root/a-behind-merge-is-bounded-and-merges-main-first, a name no task has."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repair-reads-are-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 9 and the smaller ones of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl: root's repair reads cost and say too little. (1) `my.root/problems` took 66 ms / 100 MB to 1,063 ms / 1.7 GB allocated on a store of about 25 tasks and `seon.server/task-summaries` about 560 ms / 850-915 MB a call. (2) The page names root causes only at quiescence, so a run that never ends shows 'a31 idle' and nothing links a cause to its run or task; the conflicts' content is not on the page. (3) A held merge appears twice in `my.root/problems` (:merge and :failed), and the footer counts closed entries. (4) After a successful write-back retry the task keeps its stale :seon.task/commit-error (retract it); a namespace with no file records a commit-error naming each key twice (reconcile.clj, unfiled-error). (5) A request with raw newlines answers one :seon.server/defect per line: a line that does not read as a map answers once, naming the rule one request per line, newlines escaped. Proof to write: contracted calls and bytes of one my.root/problems read on 25 tasks, flat in the conversation (a cost proof); one answer for a multi-line request. Checked 2026-11-06 at a root of 295 tasks with no runs: (1) my.root/problems 147 ms and 755 KB, seon.server/task-summaries 63 ms and 103 MB, far under the numbers above; the input that made them slow (tasks with run histories) was not staged. (5) is still so: a request of two lines sent to the socket answered two :seon.server/defect values, the second a contract error of seon.server/answer. (2), (3) and (4) were not checked."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-never-moves-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (a scratch in-agent test listing main's transactions around each eval, on 3348c59cee): every eval of any agent writes main two or three times: seon.root/record-agents writes the agent's facts (seon.task/agent-tx) whenever its view entry's observed-keys change, and the view's :running (the form an eval runs, set at its start and retracted at its end) and :status change at every eval. Of those transactions most carry no datom of their own (only :seon.tx/branch and :seon.tx/runtime): agent-tx rewrites every member, unchanged values transact to nothing, and seon.writer/commit-batch still commits each, so main's commit moves for nothing. Cost: every agent hears main moved after any agent's eval (docs/merging.md#main-moved-is-said-at-every-eval) and its page's branch views, keyed by main's commit (seon.render.ui/render-ctx), redraw (15.5M ticks against 9.2M a read, measured 2026-10-29), which is work in agents squared; seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did is red because no two of its nine reads see one main. The form running is already the eval's record on the agent's own branch, so main's copy is a second producer of one view. Still so: ran 2026-11-06 at a scripted root, main's :max-tx moved by 8 across one agent eval of `(+ 1 1)` where a root read moves it by 1. Proposed (needs the owner's go: it changes what main records of a live agent): (1) the writer commits no transaction whose datoms are all no-ops (one commit fewer, nothing lost); (2) :running and per-eval :status are root's live view (the dashboard renders root's view, as it does other live state), never main facts; main keeps an agent's spawn, end, death and stall, which change what other agents and the judge read. The page already re-runs only on what each component read (seon.render/render!, d/reads-touched?; the context session, 2026-10-30), so the 15.5M ticks a move costs is what the move sets off in every agent (hearing main moved, merging it into its branch, re-framing its walk). (1) alone leaves main moving every eval, by :running's real set and retract, so the proof stays red without (2); (1) is a change to our Datahike fork's datahike.writer/transact-batch! (an all-no-op batch answers its reports with no commit) or to seon.writer/commit-batch, a dependency change either way, which also waits for the owner's go. Ran 2026-11-06 at a root on this tree: main's :max-tx was 536871206 before one eval of (+ 1 1) in an attached agent and 536871214 after (+8); a root read moves it by 1. The transactions around it carry :seon.process/running and :seon.agent/status datoms (history shows two datoms of :seon.agent/status and one of :seon.process/running in each). The proof named below is green, and would stay green with that bug: it compares costs only of pairs of reads that saw the same main (its \"same\" filter, asserting only that some pair exists), so it does not fail when main moves at every read. The input that shows the bug is the :max-tx of main across one agent eval."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-dies-starting-answers-its-attach"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-01 (jolt/search-index-inherited, a contract error in seon.agent/schema-moved during begin-run!): an agent that threw in its start (seon.agent/init!) was logged by root (\"a forked child threw out of its code: clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo seon.agent/schema-moved invalid-input\") and the test's attach (seon.agent-test/in-agent, through seon.server's attach) waited forever: the fork server idle in its run loop (its fiber dump), no agent process, the test's shard at 0% CPU for 10 minutes until it was killed. AGENTS section 4: every wait ends with its value or a typed error; the producer that owed the answer is the agent's start, and its death is a fact root holds. Correct: a forked agent's throw before its hello is the spawn's answer (seon.root's answer-spawn already answers a death before the hello: find why this one is not taken, a death by throw in init! against one by exit), so the attach answers the error. Reproduce: an agent whose init! throws (a test environment whose begin-run! throws), and its attach answering :dead or the error within the test's run, never a stall. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.root/end-agent answers the spawn of an agent that ends before its hello (answer-spawn, with the death, src/seon/root.clj); an agent whose init! throws was not staged, so whether this attach still hangs is not known. No test is named."
