 {:seon.task/name "a-running-root-spawns-the-binary-it-was-built-as"
  :seon.task/plan "Also seen 2026-11-05: a root built before 36498f1806 spawned its vector helper while the main tree was on a branch without :seon.config.web/utc-offset; the helper read that checkout config, was refused, and left five :seon.vector/helper-died entries that stay after the next root starts. Seen 2026-11-05 in the standing store's problems before its erase, 32 entries of 5 kinds: target/seon ended before its model loaded (exit 1), the config does not satisfy :seon.config/config, with keys missing or unknown (:seon.config.store/max-value-chars, :seon.config.render/similar, :seon.config.native/sqlite-lib). Cause not confirmed, the likely one: a root spawns its model child by the path target/seon, and building another branch in that tree replaces the binary under it, so the child and the root's config disagree. Correct: a process a root spawns is the root's own build (its binary held open, or named by generation), so no rebuild reaches it; seon.check-test/a-stale-binary-is-refused is this class's neighbour. Proof to write: replacing the file at the path leaves a running root's next child its own build. Checked 2026-11-06 by reading: seon.vector/start! (src/seon/vector.clj) still spawns its helper by the path (cfg/checkout)/target/seon; nothing names or holds a generation for it. Not run."
