 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root's stop parks on the drain and an agent's end on its socket's close, with no bound (`seon.root/stop!`, `seon.agent-proc/stop!`); a child's exit is an event (`seon.fork-server`'s :exits channel, jolt.io-poller's exit-chan). What is left is one clock, which docs/clocks.md#time-decides-owed-its-owners-fix names here: `seon.root/start!` passes Flow's :stop-timeout-ms, :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms, what flow/stop waits for a proc's exit; a proc still running is named in stop!'s :graph, printed on ROOT STOPPED. Correct: a proc that does not exit is a recorded problem with no time deciding. Whether Flow can stop with no bound is not checked; it is Flow's mechanism (docs/flow.md). Deletes :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms. No proof is written for it."
  :seon.task/kind :work} {:seon.task/name "a-boot-imports-main-before-it-reads-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Schemas too (2026-11-08): the first agent on 7888 after batch3 restarted root failed every model call with seon.model/request-body invalid-input (:call, which main's new :seon.model/request admits): root held main's stored schema registry from before that boot's import brought the new entry; a second bin/update fixed it. So the boot imports main first and only then reads config AND builds the schema registry agents fork with; one proof each. What happened (2026-11-08, found by Context): after 191e4b56ba merged a new key read at root's start (:seon.config.web/streams-buf, a :bind/ buffer), bin/update's root refused to start: \":seon.config.web/streams-buf is set nowhere\". The cause: seon.root's boot loads main's stored program (seon.program/load-all!) and holds main's stored config entries (seon.program/hold-config!) before anything else reads config, and only after the graph starts does seon.root/import-source! bring main's program up to the merged source, through the :source effect and the writer. So between a merge and that import, the binary (built from the merged tree) and main's stored entries disagree, and any key the merge added that root reads at start is missing. The prove could not see it: tests run on the world (docs/testing.md#testing-in-the-world), never on a store a merge has not yet been imported into. Context booted once with --set to get past it.

The class: anything root reads before the import (a config key, a schema entry, a var root's start calls) can be newer in the binary than in main's store. A per-key guard, or making the new key optional, fixes one key and leaves the class.

Correct: the boot imports main's source before it reads anything from main. At boot root is the store's only process (seon.root's boot opens the store before the fork server forks), and seon.root/seed-program! already imports the source straight into the store, with no graph and no writer (fx/read-source, then source/tx-of, then d/transact). The boot runs the same import (the changed files only, seon.source/refresh-tx, against the :seon.root/source fact's tree) before load-all! and hold-config!, so main's program and config at boot are the merged tree's. import-source! after start then finds the digest equal and imports nothing (its own rule), so nothing is imported twice. Replaces: the gap, and the --set workaround. Deletes nothing else. Cost: the import moves earlier, it does not grow (a refresh of what the merge changed; a boot after no merge reads the tree's digest and writes nothing).

Checks (Context, 2026-11-08):
- What it imports from: bin/update fast-forwards the merges tree (:seon.config.source/root) to main before starting root, but a plain bin/root start or bin/root next does not, so that tree can be behind main or hold Seon's write-back commits not yet on main. The boot imports only when the tree's HEAD is main's commit or holds it; otherwise it imports nothing and records why, as data, never writing an older tree over main.
- A bad file never stops the boot: a merged file that does not analyze, or a config entry the schema refuses (seon.root/unimportable, seon.program/config-refused), is a problem fact naming the file, and root boots on what main's store held. A dead root is the outcome this task exists to end.
- The import reads :seon.config.source/root, dirs and branch before any config is held; they are :seon.config/place keys, read from the built text (docs/config.md#a-process-holds-its-branchs-config), and the boot's docstring says so.

Owners: seon.root's boot is Optimization's (storage, merges) and Context's (config); Web found it with its merge and can build it if they agree.

Proofs, red first: a root booted on a store whose main lacks a config key the binary reads at start (the merged tree adds it) starts, holds that key's value from the merged entries, and its import after start writes nothing; a boot with nothing merged since the last import writes nothing at boot; a boot whose import refuses one file still starts and names the file; a boot whose source tree does not hold main's commit imports nothing and says why."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-boot-after-a-merge-reads-the-merged-config
                      seon.root-test/a-boot-with-nothing-merged-imports-nothing
                      seon.root-test/a-boot-whose-import-refuses-a-file-still-starts
                      seon.root-test/a-boot-imports-no-tree-behind-main}}
]
